WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:05.440
There are often situations where using Holstein volumetrics

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:06.879
isn't desirable.

3
00:00:06.879 --> 00:00:09.640
Traditionally, we could achieve these localized volumetric

4
00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:12.759
effects using things such as fog cards.

5
00:00:12.759 --> 00:00:15.199
Thankfully, we can still use similar techniques

6
00:00:15.199 --> 00:00:18.109
to achieve the same sort of effect.

7
00:00:18.109 --> 00:00:19.589
So if we jump back to the editor,

8
00:00:19.589 --> 00:00:22.910
now I have the lit atmos level open,

9
00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:24.230
which should look like this.

10
00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:26.620
And the first thing that I want to take a look at

11
00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:30.300
is this ground plane here on, or I guess more specifically,

12
00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:31.699
the fog cube.

13
00:00:31.699 --> 00:00:34.539
Now, if you're trying to select this and it doesn't as a pro

14
00:00:34.539 --> 00:00:38.259
tip, if you press the T key, the TK

15
00:00:38.259 --> 00:00:41.700
is recognizing transparency or translucency,

16
00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:44.740
allowing you to select those transparent objects.

17
00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:46.979
So if you're trying to select and it's just selecting

18
00:00:46.979 --> 00:00:50.060
the water plane, simply press the TK

19
00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:52.460
to enable it so that the T is in Tango.

20
00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:53.979
And we should be able to select it.

21
00:00:53.979 --> 00:00:57.810
If not, go to your outliner and select your fog cube.

22
00:00:57.810 --> 00:00:58.289
All right.

23
00:00:58.289 --> 00:01:00.689
So if I just raise this cube up,

24
00:01:00.689 --> 00:01:03.130
you'll notice that it is interacting

25
00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:05.290
with our volume metrics.

26
00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:07.450
So how do we do that?

27
00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:10.530
So in the details panel of the fog cube,

28
00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:13.409
I'm going to take a look at element 0 in our materials,

29
00:01:13.409 --> 00:01:16.969
simply double click it, and this will open up

30
00:01:16.969 --> 00:01:18.609
our material instance.

31
00:01:18.609 --> 00:01:22.010
Now, in the parent, underneath General,

32
00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:26.629
I'm going to double click this mCheapGroundFog,

33
00:01:26.629 --> 00:01:29.859
and this will open up our parent material.

34
00:01:29.859 --> 00:01:32.819
Now, a quick note for a more detailed step

35
00:01:32.819 --> 00:01:35.540
by step guide on creating this material,

36
00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:37.620
please refer to the class slides.

37
00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:39.739
This video will not cover walking

38
00:01:39.739 --> 00:01:42.459
through the process of doing this step by step,

39
00:01:42.459 --> 00:01:46.030
but we have included in the slides for you.

40
00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:47.790
So when it comes to making this material,

41
00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:51.150
fundamentally, there's nothing really special about the way

42
00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:53.430
that this material has been constructed,

43
00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:56.069
and particularly what we're referring to here

44
00:01:56.069 --> 00:02:01.549
is we're not using special nodes or volumetric only

45
00:02:01.549 --> 00:02:03.030
parameters within the material.

46
00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:07.069
This is, generally speaking, just material creation 101.

47
00:02:07.069 --> 00:02:08.949
But there are a couple settings that you

48
00:02:08.949 --> 00:02:12.189
need to take note of in order for your material

49
00:02:12.189 --> 00:02:15.949
to actually interact with the volumetric fog.

50
00:02:15.949 --> 00:02:19.189
And to do that, so you can select the main parent

51
00:02:19.189 --> 00:02:21.349
material here, or just click anywhere

52
00:02:21.349 --> 00:02:24.259
in the empty space of the graph.

53
00:02:24.259 --> 00:02:26.139
And if we jump over to the details panel,

54
00:02:26.139 --> 00:02:29.300
you'll see material domain is set to volume,

55
00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:32.460
and blend mode is set to additive.

56
00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:34.740
Now, when you change the material domain,

57
00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:37.659
you'll notice that you lose a lot of the material features,

58
00:02:37.659 --> 00:02:40.500
and that is by nature the design of it.

59
00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:42.819
So what we want to do is apply whatever

60
00:02:42.819 --> 00:02:45.259
we're doing within our extinction.

61
00:02:45.259 --> 00:02:47.259
That should be familiar, because if you remember

62
00:02:47.259 --> 00:02:49.979
from our volumetric fog parameters,

63
00:02:49.979 --> 00:02:52.259
we do have the extinction scale.

64
00:02:52.259 --> 00:02:55.340
So this basically allows us to plug in our logic, which,

65
00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:56.699
again, is this is all covered

66
00:02:56.699 --> 00:02:58.259
in the slides of how to build this,

67
00:02:58.259 --> 00:03:02.259
into the extinction parameter of the material.

68
00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:06.909
Volume, additive, and then plug it in.

69
00:03:06.909 --> 00:03:11.020
All right, we'll go ahead and close that out.

70
00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:14.819
So now that we can see that the fog is basically working,

71
00:03:14.819 --> 00:03:17.060
it's interacting with our scene,

72
00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:21.180
and if I reopen the material instance,

73
00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:22.900
and again, I would recommend that,

74
00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:24.340
play with some of these parameters,

75
00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:26.340
just check them and adjust the settings,

76
00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:27.860
but you'll see that we can

77
00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:29.300
adjust various different things.

78
00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:32.300
So if I change opacity to something like 0.1,

79
00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:33.460
you'll see that it lessens,

80
00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:36.979
and then I can move this volume down.

81
00:03:36.979 --> 00:03:40.419
One other quick note to make mention here

82
00:03:40.419 --> 00:03:42.699
is that the actor that we've added

83
00:03:42.699 --> 00:03:44.699
in our scene here is just a cube.

84
00:03:44.699 --> 00:03:46.460
So I could go over to the place actors

85
00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:49.500
and just type in cube, drop in a cube,

86
00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:51.780
and apply that material.

87
00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:52.740
One of the thing, though,

88
00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:56.199
is because this is actual geometry,

89
00:03:56.199 --> 00:03:58.780
if you intend on your players kind of moving around

90
00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:01.000
in this space or interacting with it,

91
00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:04.219
you probably want to disable collision on this volume

92
00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:06.340
otherwise it will basically react

93
00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:09.039
like any other piece of geometry in your level.

94
00:04:09.039 --> 00:04:13.139
So to do that, go into the actor details panel,

95
00:04:13.139 --> 00:04:13.979
and if you scroll down,

96
00:04:13.979 --> 00:04:16.060
you can see collision presets is set to default.

97
00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:17.939
Just simply change this to no collision,

98
00:04:17.939 --> 00:04:20.220
and that will ensure that players can't step on,

99
00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:23.660
interact, or be blocked by it.

100
00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:26.139
Unreal Engine has another great actor

101
00:04:26.139 --> 00:04:27.220
that we can add to our scene

102
00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:30.860
for controlling more localized volumetric effects,

103
00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:33.819
and that is through a local fog volume.

104
00:04:33.819 --> 00:04:36.060
So let's go ahead and add one to our scene

105
00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:39.170
and adjust some of the properties and parameters with it.

106
00:04:39.170 --> 00:04:40.970
So I'm gonna go over to our place actors,

107
00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:44.610
and I'm just gonna search for local, local fog volume,

108
00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:47.550
and I'll drag this into our scene.

109
00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:49.589
I'll press the G key to go back to game mode,

110
00:04:49.589 --> 00:04:52.189
and something to make note of here is

111
00:04:52.189 --> 00:04:56.910
this volume is propagating and injecting this information

112
00:04:56.910 --> 00:04:59.089
into our volumetric fog.

113
00:04:59.089 --> 00:05:01.589
So if you recall, volumetric fog is based off

114
00:05:01.589 --> 00:05:03.750
of our lights where it's interacting.

115
00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:05.870
So if I were to just add this volume,

116
00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:09.670
this new volume fog, this local fog in a space,

117
00:05:09.670 --> 00:05:11.870
and it's not appearing, all we really need to do

118
00:05:11.870 --> 00:05:14.990
is just make sure we move it into our light.

119
00:05:14.990 --> 00:05:17.379
Okay, so I'm gonna move up here,

120
00:05:17.379 --> 00:05:18.899
and I'll zoom back a little bit.

121
00:05:18.899 --> 00:05:21.420
So you can see that I have this volume now

122
00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:23.139
within our scene, local fog volume,

123
00:05:23.139 --> 00:05:26.939
and I have a few properties that we wanna take note of.

124
00:05:26.939 --> 00:05:29.500
So the first one is radial fog density.

125
00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:31.300
This is exactly what it sounds like.

126
00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:33.540
So I'm gonna go ahead and just actually increase

127
00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:35.660
the size of our fog volume so we can see it

128
00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:39.689
a little bit better, move it towards the door.

129
00:05:39.689 --> 00:05:41.350
And you'll notice that radial fog density

130
00:05:41.350 --> 00:05:43.470
is set to one, and this is very similar

131
00:05:43.470 --> 00:05:47.629
to our fog parameter within our volumetric fog

132
00:05:47.629 --> 00:05:49.829
where, recall, as soon as we get really,

133
00:05:49.829 --> 00:05:51.790
really dense fog, we start to get

134
00:05:51.790 --> 00:05:53.629
kind of this obscuring happening.

135
00:05:53.629 --> 00:05:56.230
So I would recommend for radial fog density,

136
00:05:56.230 --> 00:05:59.389
taking this as something like 0.1 or 0.2,

137
00:05:59.389 --> 00:06:01.029
and you'll notice that it starts to react

138
00:06:01.029 --> 00:06:02.389
a little bit better.

139
00:06:02.389 --> 00:06:04.629
So as I move this into our scene,

140
00:06:04.629 --> 00:06:05.709
again, you can see that I'm getting

141
00:06:05.709 --> 00:06:09.180
this kind of localized fog.

142
00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:11.300
High fog density, fall off and offset,

143
00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:14.540
again, very similar to other fog controls that we have.

144
00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:17.540
We can change these properties as well.

145
00:06:17.540 --> 00:06:20.199
Scattering distribution, again, this goes hand in hand

146
00:06:20.199 --> 00:06:23.540
with the camera and the angle of the light.

147
00:06:23.540 --> 00:06:26.740
Again, if you want this more where you will see this fog

148
00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:29.100
independent of the camera's position

149
00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:31.300
relative to the light, set it closer to zero.

150
00:06:31.339 --> 00:06:33.339
If you want it more only viewable

151
00:06:33.339 --> 00:06:36.060
when you're seeing that light, closer to one.

152
00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:38.040
That makes sense.

153
00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:40.160
And then lastly, you see that we do have fog, albedo,

154
00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:41.639
and emissive, and this is great.

155
00:06:41.639 --> 00:06:45.040
So let's use our just fun hot pink debug here,

156
00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:47.759
and you can see that this does, in fact, change

157
00:06:47.759 --> 00:06:52.430
just the fog within this little volume here for it.

158
00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:54.069
All right, now one other thing,

159
00:06:54.069 --> 00:06:55.750
and you may have seen this when I first

160
00:06:55.750 --> 00:06:57.470
dropped the actor into our scene,

161
00:06:57.470 --> 00:06:59.389
but if I kind of start zooming close,

162
00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:03.230
you'll notice that it starts to clip away from the camera.

163
00:07:03.230 --> 00:07:06.269
And that is because one of the default settings

164
00:07:06.269 --> 00:07:09.230
inside the engine has it set to where

165
00:07:09.230 --> 00:07:11.069
these localize fog volumes

166
00:07:11.069 --> 00:07:14.110
only start around 2,000 Unreal units.

167
00:07:14.110 --> 00:07:16.089
Now, right now, we don't have a parameter

168
00:07:16.089 --> 00:07:18.790
in the details panel to be able to control this.

169
00:07:18.790 --> 00:07:21.750
However, we can do this through a CVAR.

170
00:07:21.750 --> 00:07:24.310
So if you're familiar with how to do that,

171
00:07:24.310 --> 00:07:26.430
we can go down to our Insert Console command,

172
00:07:26.470 --> 00:07:29.910
or we can press the tilde key and start typing.

173
00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:31.569
Or if you're unfamiliar with this,

174
00:07:31.569 --> 00:07:32.990
we have another way that we can add this

175
00:07:32.990 --> 00:07:36.110
just to our overall project itself.

176
00:07:36.110 --> 00:07:38.110
So to do that, I'm going to Window,

177
00:07:38.110 --> 00:07:40.110
I'm gonna go to Console Variables.

178
00:07:40.110 --> 00:07:41.990
I'm gonna add a console variable here,

179
00:07:41.990 --> 00:07:46.990
and I'm gonna start typing r.localFogVolume,

180
00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:51.350
and we're looking for our global start distance.

181
00:07:51.350 --> 00:07:52.269
All right, so there you can see

182
00:07:52.269 --> 00:07:54.509
that the default value of 2,000 units,

183
00:07:54.550 --> 00:07:56.709
and I'm gonna change this to something like 500

184
00:07:56.709 --> 00:07:59.709
and press Enter, and you'll notice now,

185
00:07:59.709 --> 00:08:01.829
it makes you start to see that fog,

186
00:08:01.829 --> 00:08:06.790
and it fades out only around 500 units from the camera.

187
00:08:06.790 --> 00:08:08.069
Again, the advantage here by saving it

188
00:08:08.069 --> 00:08:09.110
within the console variable,

189
00:08:09.110 --> 00:08:12.550
we can go in and adjust this as opposed to tilde,

190
00:08:12.550 --> 00:08:16.660
go retype, and do something else.

191
00:08:16.660 --> 00:08:18.139
All right, so that should cover our basics

192
00:08:18.139 --> 00:08:20.379
of how we can get fog cards,

193
00:08:20.379 --> 00:08:22.500
specifically controlling that through materials,

194
00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:28.810
as well as creating these local fog volumes.

195
00:08:28.930 --> 00:08:29.769
In the next video,

196
00:08:29.769 --> 00:08:32.090
we'll lightly cover post-processing volumes

197
00:08:32.090 --> 00:08:34.730
and some of the various properties you can change

198
00:08:34.730 --> 00:08:36.490
to adjust the look of your scene.
